 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE SWING BRIDGE. It opens around high tide to let the boats in and out of the upper harbour, and it makes a clatter when it does. The Esk gives the town its fish and, in the right light, its mood."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE 199 STEPS. Counted by every child who has ever climbed them, and there are 199, unless you count the landing. Coffins were once carried up to the churchyard, with a rest at every landing. The benches are for the living."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "STEP COUNT. 100. Halfway. The abbey is still up there. So is the wind."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "WHITBY ABBEY. Founded in 657 with St Hilda as abbess. Her cowherd, Caedmon, sang a hymn in English and became the first English poet we know by name. The ruins are from the 13th century, and the wind is older."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "DRACULA. In 1890 Bram Stoker stayed in Whitby and was inspired by the abbey, the churchyard and the harbour. In his novel the Count comes ashore from a wrecked ship as a great black dog and races up the steps."
  },
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 14,
   "text": "CAPTAIN COOK. James Cook learnt his seamanship on Whitby's coal ships, and his Endeavour was Whitby-built. The whalebone arch nearby remembers the whalers who once sailed from this harbour."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "WHITBY HARBOUR. A fishing port, a shipyard and a whaling station in its time. Its fish and chips are the best in the world, according to everybody in Whitby. The seagulls are the most skilled thieves in Yorkshire."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Bram Stoker, author of Dracula, stayed in Whitby in the summer of 1890, and the town and its abbey crept into the book.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "whitby_bram_stoker",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Bram Stoker"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "whitby_name",
   "title": "Name: Whitby",
   "text": "From Old Norse: 'by' means a farmstead or village, and 'Whit' is probably 'white', or perhaps a man called Hviti. Before the Vikings came, the Anglo-Saxons called the place Streoneshalh. Whitby is much easier to shout across a harbour."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 11,
   "plaque": "whitby_synod",
   "title": "The Synod of Whitby",
   "text": "In 664 the Synod of Whitby met at Hilda's abbey to settle when Easter should fall. King Oswiu of Northumbria chose the Roman reckoning over the Irish tradition that had come from Iona, and the English church followed Rome. A very big meeting about a calendar."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 48,
   "plaque": "whitby_ammonites",
   "title": "Snakestones",
   "text": "Legend says St Hilda cleared the headland of snakes by turning them to stone, and the coiled ammonites on the beach are what was left. Victorian sellers sometimes carved little heads on them to help the story along. Three ammonites sit on the town's coat of arms."
  },
  {
   "x": 54,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "whitby_kippers",
   "title": "Whitby Kippers",
   "text": "A kipper is a herring split open, salted and smoked over a slow wood fire. On Henrietta Street, Fortune's has smoked them in the same little smokehouse since Victorian times, and the smell finds you long before the sign does. With bread and butter, it is breakfast."
  }
 ],
